WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.630
Students say the net reads the sentence.

2
00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:07.110
Point them at the end number with the

3
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.490
slider on the left: the sentence is gone.

4
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.000
In the seminar: one symbol at a

5
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.480
time, and a number that fades before

6
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:18.040
you name vanishing gradients.

7
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:22.240
A sequence is read one symbol at a time.

8
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.670
The hidden state is the only

9
00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:26.650
memory the next symbol will see.

10
00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:29.780
Each step keeps only a

11
00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:30.920
share of that state.

12
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.320
If the share is near zero, the cue is

13
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:35.940
gone after a few empty steps.

14
00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.610
Near one, the sign of the cue is

15
00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:40.790
still there at the end.

16
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.600
That is the limit of this simple net: a

17
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:46.780
long gap washes the start away.

18
00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.250
Here is how much of the cue is left.

19
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.970
A weak memory keeps almost none, and a

20
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.990
strong memory keeps most of it.

21
00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:57.620
Quick check!

22
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.560
Three empty steps follow a cue,

23
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.600
and the memory is near zero.

24
00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.660
What is left at the end?

25
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.170
Almost none of the cue.

26
00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.040
A small memory, multiplied again and

27
00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.260
again, washes the start away.

28
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.140
Students write the end number at two

29
00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.520
slider positions before you name the idea.

30
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.300
Then they move the slider and

31
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.180
correct the prediction.

32
00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:25.270
Your turn.

33
00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.210
Does a recurrent net read the

34
00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.650
whole sequence in one look?

35
00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:31.690
Remember this.

36
00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.250
The hidden state is the memory,

37
00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:36.690
and a long gap fades it.

38
00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.300
Attention, next, looks back instead.

39
00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:42.950
Try it in your next lecture.

40
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:46.410
Predict the end number, then slide,

41
00:01:47.030 --> 00:01:48.510
then correct the prediction.
